    thinking: "**Defining the Feature**\n\nI am now focused on defining this feature request more clearly. It involves adding a new input field within the \"Protocolos\" section of the Admin panel. This field will be for a URL, linking to a specific document. The goal is to provide a user-friendly way for administrators to upload and manage these document links directly from the admin interface.\n\n\n**Investigating the Code**\n\nI\'ve moved on to examining the existing codebase, focusing on `/pages/AdminLogin.tsx`. I found that `currentProtocol.documentUrl` is already being saved, which is interesting. The `EventType` type defined in `types.ts` is connected. I am looking at lines 425-483 in the form, and am focusing on how the protocol is edited, including the existing inputs for title, icon, description, image, and base steps. I also noticed steps have a `downloadUrl` property.\n\n\n**Clarifying User Requirements**\n\nI\'m now clarifying the user\'s intent regarding the document link. The user wants the administrator to provide a document URL, and I\'m currently figuring out if this applies to the entire protocol or to each individual step. Based on the \"segun lo que requiere el usuario\" phrasing, I believe this refers to the documents associated with steps. I\'ve been reviewing the Step editor code to confirm this.\n\n\n**Analyzing Step Properties**\n\nI\'m now investigating the properties of the step editor more closely, specifically the existing inputs. I\'ve focused on how steps handle updates and removals, noting the video URL input, title, and description fields. I\'ve been looking at how updates work, noting how `handleUpdateStep` is being utilized to manage changes to titles and descriptions, and am working to understand the video URL implementation.\n\n\n**Refining Input Fields**\n\nI\'m now zeroing in on the required input fields. After considering the existing code and the user\'s intent, I\'ve decided to add a `downloadUrl` field to each step, aligning with the existing `isDownloadable` property. This approach seems logical given the structure of the Step type and the existing functionality for video URLs. I\'m also considering a toggle for `isDownloadable` for each step to give the user more control.\n\n\n**Planning the Implementation**\n\nI\'m now planning the implementation. I\'ve confirmed that `downloadUrl` is indeed part of the step object. I will add a new input field for the `downloadUrl` within the step editor, along with the `videoUrl` and other step properties, as per the user\'s intent to add a document link. The target file is `/pages/AdminLogin.tsx`. I will adjust the existing video URL input, to include an input for `downloadUrl`\n\n\n**Implementing the Download URL**\n\nI\'m now implementing the `downloadUrl` input within the step editor. I\'ve added a new input field, mirroring the structure of the existing `videoUrl` input. I am aiming to align the functionality, ensuring changes are correctly captured via `handleUpdateStep`. The placeholder text is now for a document URL, and the new input is in the correct place, within the existing grid layout.\n\n\n**Adding Protocol-Level URL**\n\nI\'ve just considered the bigger picture. I\'m now adding a document URL field at the protocol level, in addition to the step-level `downloadUrl`. I\'m putting it right below the description field, to provide comprehensive document management. This will be in `AdminLogin.tsx` too, and uses `currentProtocol.documentUrl`.\n\n\n**Interpreting User Intent**\n\nI\'m now focusing on the user\'s specific phrasing, \"segun lo que requiere el usuario\". Based on this, I need to make sure the implementation addresses both step-specific download URLs and the broader protocol document link, as I\'ve been working on, because that seems to clarify the intent. I\'m double-checking the code to ensure both needs are fully met. I will evaluate the placement of the protocol level document url.\n\n\n**Prioritizing Step Downloads**\n\nI\'m now zeroing in on the importance of download URLs at the step level, as clarified by the user\'s phrasing. I\'m prioritizing the update of the step section, following the user\'s requirements. I\'m focusing on the input field and its proper function. I will ensure a clear and user-friendly experience for adding document links within individual steps, which will take precedence.\n\n\n\n**Refining Code Interactions**\n\nI\'m now refining the code interactions, specifically within the step editor. I\'m focusing on the `onChange` handler for the `downloadUrl` input. I\'m taking a look at how it currently calls `handleUpdateStep` and am working to clean up this functionality so the call is efficient. I am making sure the state and functionality are working together to save both the URL and update the `isDownloadable` state.\n\n\n**Optimizing State Updates**\n\nI\'m now focusing on optimizing the state updates, specifically within the `handleUpdateStep` function. I see that calling this function multiple times synchronously, as is done when updating both `downloadUrl` and `isDownloadable`, might result in the second call overwriting the first due to the usage of the stale `currentProtocol` state. To address this, I\'m considering either modifying `handleUpdateStep` to accept multiple fields, creating an inline handler, or exploring if relying on the initialization of `isDownloadable` can eliminate the need for redundant calls, especially if I just update `downloadUrl`. I\'m going to look more at how `isDownloadable` is used in the app.\n\n\n**Implementing Inline Updates**\n\nI\'m now implementing the inline updates. Considering the user\'s directive to avoid refactoring, I\'m integrating the `downloadUrl` and `isDownloadable` state updates directly within the input\'s `onChange` handler. This approach avoids modifying the `handleUpdateStep` function. The code directly updates `currentProtocol` with the new `downloadUrl` value and sets `isDownloadable` based on whether the URL is present, aligning with the existing structure. It addresses the issues with multiple synchronous calls in an elegant and safe way, while minimizing changes.\n\n\n\n**Integrating Protocol-Level Link**\n\nI\'m now integrating the protocol-level Document URL into the admin interface, and I\'ve placed the input field directly after the description area within the current protocol settings. This placement ensures that the general document link is immediately accessible and logically grouped with the protocol details. The update incorporates an input for the document URL, using the existing state management for `currentProtocol`.\n\n\n**Verifying Field Placement**\n\nI\'m now focusing on the placement of the protocol-level document URL within `/pages/AdminLogin.tsx`. I see lines 437-440 are the description fields, and I intend to insert the URL input after that, which aligns with the planned integration. I\'ve re-reviewed lines 470-475 to confirm the step editor remains unchanged, and confirm the existing state management for `currentProtocol`. The placement seems correct and the code appears clean.\n\n\n**Refactoring State Management**\n\nI\'m now refactoring state management to use an inline approach. Using an inline approach allows me to directly update the state within the `onChange` handler of the `downloadUrl` input. I\'m focusing on ensuring changes are properly reflected within the state of the component. I am double-checking that both the URL and `isDownloadable` state are updated correctly.\n\nI\'m now using `multi_edit_file` with the code provided.\n\n\n**Finalizing Integration**\n\nI\'m now finalizing the integration of the download URL functionality. I\'ve ensured the inline state updates for both `downloadUrl` and `isDownloadable` are functioning correctly within the step editor. I\'m verifying the placement of the protocol-level document URL is effective, immediately after the description. I\'ve re-reviewed the entire implementation, confirming that both the step-level and protocol-level document URLs are working smoothly and adhering to all existing code standards.\n\n\n**Finalizing Code Integration**\n\nI\'m now integrating the updated code with the existing codebase to provide download URL functionality at both the step and protocol levels. I am making sure the inputs function properly, especially the inline updates for the `downloadUrl` and `isDownloadable` state for the steps. I verified that protocol-level URL integration works as intended, confirming placement after the description field. I\'ve re-reviewed everything to ensure no regressions were introduced.\n\n\n\n"
